<p>Anyone who will do this for me when needed more than deserves to be called a hero!</p>
<p>A note to non-Australian residents: the truth is we don’t see these guys as often as you think we do. All the ones that won’t kill you are completely harmless.</p>
<p>Aussies love to tease the rest of the world about the wild, wild world of nature we live in, when the encounters are actually relatively rare. Depending on where you live. And whether you have several funnel-web colonies living in your backyard, as I do. Even so, we seldom see more than 1 year, and I’ve only ever seen 2 in the house in the 20 years we’ve lived on this property.</p>
<p><span class="small-caps">FYI</span> my hero husband sometimes goes and digs them up, puts them in glass jars, and drops<span class="push-single"></span> <span class="pull-single">’</span>em off at our local wildlife park to help with the anti-venom supplies.</p>
<p>Oh dear, I’m still not doing much for tourism, am I! I’ll end with a riddle.</p>
<p>One Aussie bloke picks up another in his ute and as his friend is getting in, says,<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>Hey mate, put the sunvisor down, will ya?” His friend looks at him with widening eyes and says,<span class="push-double"></span> <span class="pull-double">“</span>No waaay!!” —Why?</p>
